You draft ONE paragraph, then stop. Its shape comes from `references/sections/` — measured
from real papers in our corpus, not from any external template. Every asserted sentence has a ledger
row naming its evidence **before** the sentence exists.

**The write contract.** `Write` and `Edit` stay absent from allowed-tools; this
skill never touches `main.tex` — you place the paragraph yourself. `Bash` was re-admitted for exactly
two purposes, and the guarantee moved from a tool gate to a behavioural contract:

1. **Read-only checks** — `py -3 scripts/draftcheck.py` (sentence counts, traceable marks and cite
   keys for the ledger, ownership regex, verb legality, Version existence, numbers-vs-table) and `py -3 scripts/build_evidence_map.py
   --check`. These write nothing.
2. **One whitelisted write path** — `py -3 scripts/state.py`, which appends to
   `references/manuscript-state.md` and to no other file (backup + disk read-back inside the script).

**Anything else Bash could write is off-contract.** No `>`, no `tee`, no heredoc, no edits to any file
outside `references/manuscript-state.md`. `scripts/check_drift.py` is the maintainer-side monitor of
this contract; a run that wrote anywhere else is a broken run, report it. For searches, **use the
`Grep` tool, not shell `grep`.**

## Rules

1. **One paragraph per turn.** Draft it, stop, wait.
2. **Structure is quoted, not invented.** The paragraph's move and version must come from a named Version
   in a `references/sections/` file (e.g. `03-<part>.md · Version 7`); the output reports it and the papers attesting it.
3. **No ledger row, no sentence — and the ledger is validated before prose exists.** Every asserted
   claim gets a row naming its evidence first; the VALIDATE step (workflow 3a) checks the finished
   ledger as a whole before any drafting starts. Evidence missing → **drop the sentence**, do not
   hedge it. A row's `warrant` cell (why THIS evidence licenses THIS claim) is a ⚠, never a gate.
4. **What a cited paper says is pasted, never recalled.** Paste the source line with `file:line`; the
   attributing verb may be no stronger than that line supports (`references/verb-ladder.md`).
   Check `references/manuscript-state.md` §C first — a line pasted and adjudicated in an earlier run
   is reused, not re-litigated.
   **Before Tier 4 is built** this field has no `references/verb-ladder.md` and no
   `references/structure-canon.md` (the verb counts and rules quoted further down are the parent
   field's, marked PARENT): attribute with `note that`, or `find that` when the pasted line itself
   states the finding, and take structural rules from `references/structure-canon.skeleton.md` as
   unmeasured guidance, never as this field's measured norms.
5. **Draft only, write only through `state.py`.** The manuscript is never written; the sole write
   path is the behavioural contract above. Register is not this skill's call — hand off to
   `/{{FIELD}}-lit`.

## Workflow

| # | Step | Calls |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | **INTAKE** — target section, ¶ number, what it must say. Map the section to its directory using the table below. Read `references/manuscript-state.md` (which Versions this manuscript already used/rejected; cached source lines) **if it exists** — before manuscript onboarding it does not, and there is simply no prior state to carry | 0–1 (once per session) |
| 1a | **SKELETON** — read `references/sections/<chapter>/00-skeleton.md`; its **router** says which part file this paragraph needs, and its typing appendix says how binding each part is. Recommend ONE Version, name one or two alternates — never a menu | 1 (once per chapter) |
| 1b | **VERSIONS** — read the **one** part file the router names; pick a Version | 1 |
| 2a | **EVIDENCE INDEX** — read `references/evidence-map.md` (the cached section/table/figure index). If `py -3 scripts/build_evidence_map.py --check` says STALE, rebuild it first. **Absent before manuscript onboarding**: with no manuscript there is nothing to index, so own-result claims cannot be `SUPPORTED` and the ledger must mark them `ORPHAN` rather than pretend | 0–1 (once per session) |
| 2b | **NOTES** — one Grep for this ¶'s cite-keys via `references/notes-index.md`; skip keys already in state §C. **Skipped entirely when that file does not exist** — see the attribution guard | 0–1 |
| 3a | **VALIDATE LEDGER** — the ledger is complete: every planned assertion has a row, every row has a pasted source line, ownership ran on every line (`draftcheck.py own`), verbs are ladder-legal. **No prose until this passes** | 0–1 |
| 3b | **DRAFT** — one paragraph; joins per `../{{FIELD}}-lit/references/register.md` §8; intro/abstract paragraphs also emit PROMISES rows | 0 |
| 3c | **MOVE-CHECK** — re-read the chosen Version's `Pattern`/`Use it when` against the draft: did the paragraph execute the moves of the Version selected? Fail → revise → re-check (the validator is the Version file itself; max 2 rounds) | 0 |
| 4 | **SELF-CHECK** — the numbered questions below, each answered by a slot; `py -3 scripts/draftcheck.py para` supplies the counts | 1 |
| 5 | **STATE** — `py -3 scripts/state.py use …` for the Version placed; `state.py cache …` for every source line pasted this run | 1 |
| 6 | **HAND OFF** | 0 |

**Budget: ≤8 calls for the first paragraph of a session (0+1a+1b+2a+2b+3a+4+5), 3–5 after.** Step 1b
is per paragraph, so the floor is 1, never 0. When the budget is tight, cut 2b (reuse state §C), never
3a or 4. Subagents: 0 in normal drafting; a `claim-verifier` fork is allowed only under
attribution-guard rule 2.

**Degrees of freedom are graded by fragility, not uniform.** The ledger and the two scripts' command
lines are **low freedom** — exact formats, do not improvise. Version selection is **medium** — the
router recommends, the chapter's typing appendix and the author's history (state §A/§B) inform, you
argue the pick. The prose itself is **high freedom** — the Version constrains the move, never the
sentences; that separation is what the uniformity gate protects.

**Read the skeleton, then exactly one part file — never the whole chapter.** The sections layer is
split precisely so that drafting one paragraph costs one skeleton plus one part file, not the lot
(sizes: `registry.json` → `sections`). Each `00-skeleton.md` carries a router: it turns "I am
writing ¶3 of <chapter>" into "open `<chapter>/02-<part>.md`".

Routers differ by chapter because the corpus does. A chapter whose parts run in a measured order
gets a **position table** (¶ number in, part file out); a chapter organised by what a paragraph
does rather than by where it sits gets a **Version→file** table; a front block (an abstract) may
get a **sentence-position** table. Read the skeleton's own §1–§2 and follow what it says.

**That contract dictates where a section lives, and it is not cosmetic.** A part file has to be
self-sufficient for writing its part, so anything you need *while writing* — the ordering rule, the
paragraph budget, the which-Version-first split — sits beside the Versions it governs, never in the
skeleton (parent build: `## The usual order` / `## Where it sits` sat in 6 of the Introduction's 8
part files and never in its `00-skeleton.md`; `## Paragraph budget` likewise). The skeleton carries
only what is true *across* parts — the arc, the router, the chapter-wide length distribution,
corpus-wide disagreements, chapter-wide zeros, and `For this manuscript`.

**The test when building a new chapter:** *if someone opened only this part file and wrote the part,
would this section have been needed?* Yes → part file. Only useful when comparing parts → skeleton.

The files that are **never read whole** are `references/records/*.md` — one per chapter, the raw
paragraph-by-paragraph reading notes, often hundreds of KB. Use the `Grep` tool for `^### <Key>` to get an
offset, then read that block. In normal drafting you read them **zero times**; they serve `--outline`
and evidence re-checks only. They are the evidence layer; `sections/` is what was induced from them.

Two subdirectories are **not** part of that set. `records/_shards/` holds the per-agent slices that
were concatenated into the chapter files (verified block-for-block identical — pure duplicates, kept
only for provenance). A `records/_superseded/` folder exists only in the parent (its two early
tagged rounds) — a stamped instance has none unless it runs a tagged round; labelling during reading
locks the induction into a taxonomy chosen before anything was read.

