**On McCloskey's objection.** *Economical Writing* tells authors to "spurn the received outline" —
and a corpus-induced Versions system is, by construction, a received outline
(the parent quoted McCloskey 1985 for this; adjudicated as `structure-canon.md` D7). The answer this
skill commits to: the layer is **descriptive** (what 60 published papers did, with counts), never
prescriptive (306 Versions per slot is the opposite of one template), and the uniformity gate exists
precisely to stop corpus conformity from producing uniform prose. The navigate-don't-retrieve
architecture itself is independently validated (arXiv 2604.14572: routers as classifiers over a
skill tree beat retrieve-then-read on both accuracy and token cost).

**¶1 of the introduction is an opening in 40 of the 41 empirical papers**, not a task definition.
Paragraph length over 603 introduction paragraphs from all 60 papers: mean 5.37, P25 4, median 5,
P75 7, P90 8, **P95 10 — over 10 sentences, split the paragraph.** Three consecutive paragraphs with
identical sentence counts occur in 17 of 484 real windows = 3.5%; that is the uniformity gate. Two
consecutive is 85 of 543 = 15.7%, one window in six — **ordinary, never flag it.** The uniformity gate
is chapter-independent (it measures your prose, not the corpus's), so it applies everywhere.
(Recounted 2026-08-26 by `pipeline/recount.py` after its dead file pointer was
fixed; the previously published figures were 609 · 17/489 · 88/549 = 16.0% — every
percentile and the mean survived the recount unchanged.)
