## Claim → evidence tracer

Parses the onboarded manuscript (`manuscript_tex` in field-config.yaml) — LaTeX: the
`\section`/`\label{sec:}` tree, each `\input{…}` table with its `\label{tab:}` and caption,
`\includegraphics` + `\label{fig:}`, all `\ref{}`/`\cite*{}`; a Markdown manuscript needs its parser
written at onboarding (`build_evidence_map.py` exits with a message until then).
**Traceable sentence** = contains a numeral, percentage, comparative, or directional verb; institutional
description and definitions are `N/A`.

Status set: `SUPPORTED` · `SUPPORTED-UNCITED` · `ORPHAN` · `MISMATCH` · `EXTERNAL` · `N/A`.
**`ORPHAN` and `MISMATCH` block** — the sentence comes back removed, with its row shown.
The output prints a **six-status tally, all six every time** — a status that fired nothing prints `0`,
so "checked and clean" and "never checked" cannot look alike. `py -3 scripts/draftcheck.py numbers`
pre-screens draft numerals against the referenced `.tex` table; claim → R/Stata output stays with
`/audit-reproducibility`.

## Promise ledger — the tracer's other direction (Shapiro's contract)

The claim ledger asks *what evidence backs this sentence*. The promise ledger asks the dual: **which
body section discharges what this introduction/abstract sentence promises** (`structure-canon.md` Q7 —
Shapiro: the final introduction *"is a contract between you and the reader"*; the undischarged promise
is the bait-and-switch Head names as a top rejection cause).

When the drafted paragraph belongs to the introduction or abstract, every forward-looking assertion
("we show…", "we exploit…", "we find…") gets a `PROMISES` row:

```
promise (as drafted) | discharged by (sec:/tab:/fig: from evidence-map) | status
```

Status: `DISCHARGED` (the section/table exists and carries it) · `PENDING` (planned but not yet in
main.tex — legal while drafting, listed) · `UNBACKED` (nothing in the manuscript or the plan carries
it — **the sentence comes back removed**, same rule as ORPHAN). `--trace` runs the same check over a
finished intro/abstract wholesale: claim→table (the old half) plus promise→section (this half).

## Uniformity gate (calibrated, not guessed)

From ¶3 on, compare the last three paragraphs on sentence count / first-sentence type / last-sentence type.
**≥2 of 3 axes identical across all three → `UNIFORM-SHAPE`**; redraft one using a *different attested
version of the same move*.

<!-- PARENT-BLOCK tier=3 -->
> **[PARENT — economics/finance uniformity calibration. Rewrite from this instance's own measurements at Tier 3 and delete this block's markers; check_drift fails at Tier 3 while it remains.]**

Calibration over all 60 papers: three consecutive paragraphs with identical
sentence counts occur in **17 of 484 real windows = 3.5%**.

**Do not flag a matched pair.** Two consecutive paragraphs of equal length occur in 85 of 543 windows
= 15.7% — one window in six. Only the three-window is rare enough to mean anything.
<!-- /PARENT-BLOCK -->

This works with the humanizer's rule against uniform sentence shapes rather than colliding with it: the skeleton constrains
**move order**, never sentence micro-architecture. Uniform move order is what the corpus does; uniform
sentence shape is what AI does. `py -3 scripts/draftcheck.py para` supplies the sentence counts; the two
type axes stay judgment (the introduction chapter's `00-skeleton.md`, once Tier 3 writes it, holds
the first-sentence taxonomy).

## Paragraph completeness — the `PARA` slot (argument, not evidence)

The claim ledger certifies every sentence is *sourced*; this slot certifies the paragraph's *argument
closes* — orthogonal, both required (`structure-canon.md` §3). Three questions, answered per draft:

1. **C-C-C** (Mensh & Kording): does the first sentence give context, the last a conclusion — or is a
   reader left asking *"Why was I told that?"* or *"So what?"*
2. **Single point, topics closed**: one point per paragraph; a topic opened resolves before the next
   opens. Two points = a disguised paragraph break.
3. **Results paragraphs only — question asked, question answered**: the opener poses, the closer
   answers. A results paragraph that ends on a coefficient with no verdict fails this.

Verdicts are advisory (✅/⚠ with one clause of reasoning), printed every time — a PARA line that
found nothing prints three ✅, never disappears.

## When drafting a LONG introduction — the cyclicity licence

**TO-MEASURE ({{FIELD}}).** The parent measured this on 18 hand-tagged introductions: 13 of 15 with
≥10 paragraphs revisit an already-used part; 0 of 3 short ones do. **That is the parent's field, not
this one.** Detecting a re-entry needs a per-paragraph move tag — the layer-A tagging — so if
`structure-norms.json`'s `A_tagged18` is `null` in this instance, there is no cyclicity number here
and this section must not assert one. Until it is measured: allow re-entry in a long intro without
flagging it, and do not flag its absence either. Route a revisit paragraph back through the same part
file and say so in the SKELETON line (`part: <part> (2nd pass)`).

